Chapter: Audio

 The Audio effect is the right to make the machine emit sound. It is a
 bare marker here, the way Microphone and Display are: the operations
 that use it are not declared here.

 They are not declared here because playing a note means driving the HDA
 controller's stream registers over MMIO, and MMIO is codex.os, which a
 foreword chapter may not depend on (Library Rule 1). A chapter that
 wants to play something cites Kernel chapter HdaAudio and gets
 definitions that drive the real codec. A chapter that does not gets
 CDX3002 on the name -- the truth: it asked for something it did not
 bring.

 Declaring the operations here anyway is the mistake this chapter exists
 to avoid, and it has been made before: Microphone once declared listen
 and is-quiet with no handler anywhere in the tree, so a call type-checked
 clean and died at emit with CDX2040. A marker that promises nothing keeps
 that promise.

 Audio is the authority to SPEAK, and it is deliberately not the same
 authority as Microphone, which is the right to LISTEN. Neither implies
 the other and a program is expected to ask for what it does.

 We say:

Section: Definitions

  effect Audio where
